JAVA VARIABLES

1. What is a Variable?
A variable is a named memory location used to store data.
The value of a variable can be used and changed during program execution.

Example:
int age = 20;
Here:
int = data type
age = variable name
20 = value

2. Syntax of Variable Declaration
dataType variableName = value;

Example:
int marks = 85;
String name = "Rahul";
double percentage = 85.5;

3. Variable Declaration and Initialization
Declaration means creating a variable.

Example:
int number;
Initialization means assigning a value to a variable.

Example:
number = 10;
Both can be done together:
int number = 10;

4. Rules for Naming Variables
• A variable name can contain letters, digits, underscore (_) and dollar
  sign ($).
• It cannot begin with a digit.
• Spaces are not allowed.
• Java keywords cannot be used as variable names.
• Variable names are case-sensitive.

Example:
int studentAge = 20;
int marks1 = 90;
int _number = 5;

Invalid examples:
int 1marks = 90;
int student age = 20;

5. Types of Variables in Java
A. Local Variable
   A variable declared inside a method or block.

Example:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age = 20;
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

B. Instance Variable
   A variable declared inside a class but outside methods.
   Each object has its own copy.

Example:
class Student {
    int marks = 80;
}

C. Static Variable
A variable declared using the static keyword.
It belongs to the class and is shared by all objects.

Example:
class Student {
    static String college = "ABC College";
}

6. Example Program
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age = 20;
        String name = "Rahul";
        double marks = 85.5;

        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(age);
        System.out.println(marks);
    }
}

Output:
Rahul
20
85.5

7. Changing Variable Values

Example:
int number = 10;
number = 25;
System.out.println(number);

Output:
25

Conclusion:
Variables are used to store and manage data in Java.
Every variable has a data type, a name, and a value.

JAVA CONDITIONAL STATEMENTS

1. What is a Conditional Statement?
A conditional statement is used to make decisions in a program.
It executes different blocks of code depending on whether a condition
is true or false.

Java provides the following conditional statements:

• if statement
• if-else statement
• if-else-if ladder
• nested if statement
• switch statement

2. if Statement
   The if statement executes a block of code only when the condition is  true.

Syntax:
if (condition) {
    // code to execute
}

Example:
int age = 20;
if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Eligible to vote");
}

Output:
Eligible to vote

3. if-else Statement
   The if-else statement executes one block if the condition is true
and another block if the condition is false.

Example:
int number = 5;
if (number % 2 == 0) {
    System.out.println("Even number");
} else {
    System.out.println("Odd number");
}

Output:
Odd number

4. if-else-if Ladder
   It is used when we need to check multiple conditions.

Example:
int marks = 75;
if (marks >= 90) {
    System.out.println("Grade A");
} else if (marks >= 75) {
    System.out.println("Grade B");
} else if (marks >= 50) {
    System.out.println("Grade C");
} else {
    System.out.println("Fail");
}

Output:
Grade B

5. Nested if Statement
   An if statement inside another if statement is called a nested if.

Example:
int age = 20;
boolean hasID = true;
if (age >= 18) {
    if (hasID) {
        System.out.println("Entry allowed");
    }
}

Output:
Entry allowed

6. switch Statement
   The switch statement selects one block from multiple options.

Example:
int day = 2;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
        break;
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Wednesday");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Invalid day");
}

Output:
Tuesday

Explanation:
• case specifies a possible value.
• break stops execution of the switch.
• default runs when no case matches.

7. Ternary Operator
   The ternary operator is a short form of if-else.

Syntax:
condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse;

Example:
int age = 20;
String result = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
System.out.println(result);

Output:
Adult

8. Comparison Operators

==  Equal to
!=  Not equal to
>   Greater than
<   Less than
>=  Greater than or equal to
<=  Less than or equal to

9. Logical Operators

&&  Logical AND
||  Logical OR
!   Logical NOT

Example:
int age = 20;
if (age >= 18 && age <= 60) {
    System.out.println("Eligible");
}

Output:
Eligible

Conclusion:
Conditional statements control the flow of a Java program.
They help a program make decisions using conditions and operators.

JAVA LOOPS

1. What is a Loop?
A loop is a programming structure used to execute a block of code
repeatedly until a condition becomes false.
Loops reduce code repetition and make programs easier to manage.

Java provides these loops:
• for loop
• while loop
• do-while loop
• enhanced for loop

2. for Loop
The for loop is used when we know how many times we want to
execute a block of code.

Syntax:
for (initialization; condition; update) {
    // statements
}

Example:
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

Output:
1
2
3
4
5

Explanation:
int i = 1
Initializes the loop variable.
i <= 5
Checks the condition before every iteration.
i++
Increases the value of i by 1.

3. while Loop
The while loop executes as long as the condition is true.

Syntax:
while (condition) {
    // statements
}

Example:
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}

Output:
1
2
3
4
5

The condition is checked before the loop body executes.
If the condition is false initially, the loop will not run.

4. do-while Loop
The do-while loop executes the block at least once.
The condition is checked after the loop body.

Syntax:
do {
    // statements
} while (condition);

Example:
int i = 1;
do {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
} while (i <= 5);

Output:
1
2
3
4
5

5. Difference Between while and do-while
while loop:
• Checks condition first.
• May execute zero times.

do-while loop:
• Executes the body first.
• Executes at least once.

6. Enhanced for Loop
The enhanced for loop is used to access elements of an array
or collection one by one.

Example:
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};
for (int number : numbers) {
    System.out.println(number);
}

Output:
10
20
30
40

7. break Statement
The break statement immediately terminates the loop.

Example:
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) {
        break;
    }
    System.out.println(i);
}

Output:
1
2

8. continue Statement
The continue statement skips the current iteration and
moves to the next iteration.

Example:
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) {
        continue;
    }
    System.out.println(i);
}

Output:
1
2
4
5

9. Nested Loop
A loop inside another loop is called a nested loop.

Example:
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 2; j++) {
        System.out.println(i + " " + j);
    }
}

Output:
1 1
1 2
2 1
2 2
3 1
3 2

Conclusion:
Loops are used to repeat statements efficiently.
The for, while, do-while, and enhanced for loops are commonly
used in Java programming.

CSS BASICS

1. What is CSS?
CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets.
CSS is used to style and design HTML webpages.
It controls colors, fonts, spacing, borders, layouts,
   and the appearance of webpage elements.
HTML creates the structure of a webpage.
CSS makes the webpage attractive and organized.

2. CSS Syntax
selector {
    property: value;
}

Example:
h1 {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 30px;
}

Explanation:
h1 = selector
color = property
blue = value

3. Types of CSS
A. Inline CSS
CSS is written directly inside an HTML element.

Example:
<p style="color: red;">Hello World</p>

B. Internal CSS
CSS is written inside the <style> tag in the HTML head.

Example:
<head>
    <style>
        p {
            color: green;
        }
    </style>
</head>

C. External CSS
CSS is written in a separate .css file.

Example:
style.css
p {
    color: purple;
}
Link the CSS file in HTML:
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
External CSS is commonly used because it keeps HTML and CSS separate.

5. CSS Colors
CSS supports different ways to specify colors.

Example:
h1 {
    color: red;
}
p {
    color: #0000ff;
}
body {
    background-color: lightyellow;
}

6. CSS Text and Font Properties
Example:
p {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: bold;
    text-align: center;
    text-decoration: underline;
}
font-family specifies the font.
font-size specifies the text size.
font-weight controls boldness.
text-align controls alignment.
text-decoration adds decoration.

7. CSS Background
Example:
body {
    background-color: #eaf2ff;
}
.box {
    background-image: url("background.jpg");
    background-size: cover;
}

8. CSS Border
Example:
div {
    border: 2px solid black;
    border-radius: 10px;
}

border adds an outline.
border-radius makes corners rounded.

9. CSS Margin and Padding
Margin:
Space outside an element.
Padding:
Space inside an element, between content and border.

Example:
.box {
    margin: 20px;
    padding: 15px;
}

10. CSS Width and Height
Example:
.box {
    width: 300px;
    height: 150px;
}
These properties control the size of an element.

11. CSS Display Property
Example:
div {
    display: block;
}

Common display values:
block - starts on a new line.
inline - stays within the same line.
inline-block - inline but supports width and height.
none - hides the element.

12. Simple CSS Example

HTML:
<h1 class="title">Welcome to CSS</h1>
<p class="message">CSS makes webpages beautiful.</p>

CSS:
.title {
    color: purple;
    text-align: center;
}
.message {
    color: navy;
    font-size: 18px;
    background-color: lightblue;
    padding: 15px;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

Conclusion:
CSS is used to control the design and layout of webpages.
It works with HTML to create attractive, readable,
and well-organized websites.
